1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,231
Since January 2026, steel entering
the EU carries a carbon price.

2
00:00:04,231 --> 00:00:09,615
But the proof of how clean it is still
travels by email, in spreadsheets.

3
00:00:09,965 --> 00:00:15,350
Without verified data, the importer pays
the EU default: 2.43 tonnes of CO₂ per

4
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:18,427
tonne of Turkish hot-rolled
steel, plus a mark-up.

5
00:00:18,427 --> 00:00:23,812
Yet 70% of Turkish steel comes from electric
arc furnaces, far cleaner than that.

6
00:00:24,162 --> 00:00:30,315
A clean mill is priced like a dirty one, so
there's no reward for cutting emissions.

7
00:00:30,315 --> 00:00:34,162
And nothing stops the same
low-carbon tonnes being sold twice.

8
00:00:34,512 --> 00:00:38,358
Here's Anatolia Green Steel, a
fictional electric-arc mill in İzmir.

9
00:00:38,358 --> 00:00:42,204
Its compliance officer loads the
month's gas and electricity invoices,

10
00:00:42,204 --> 00:00:44,127
production report and meter log.

11
00:00:44,127 --> 00:00:47,588
The AI extraction step, here
with recorded sample output,

12
00:00:47,588 --> 00:00:52,973
pulls out every quantity and links
each one to the page it came from.

13
00:00:52,973 --> 00:00:54,512
She confirms each value.

14
00:00:54,512 --> 00:00:56,819
The AI never writes anything on-chain.

15
00:00:57,169 --> 00:01:01,785
The CBAM calculator gives 0.356
tonnes of CO₂ per tonne of steel.

16
00:01:01,785 --> 00:01:07,169
Our anomaly check compares it with the
plant's own 12-month history and flags one

17
00:01:07,169 --> 00:01:09,477
value for the verifier to review.

18
00:01:09,827 --> 00:01:15,212
Submitting commits only a salted Merkle
root and a report hash to the chain.

19
00:01:15,212 --> 00:01:17,519
Competitors see nothing about the process.

20
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:20,483
An accredited verifier,
registered on-chain,

21
00:01:20,483 --> 00:01:23,945
checks the evidence against those
commitments, reviews the flag,

22
00:01:23,945 --> 00:01:27,791
and signs twice: with an
Ethereum key, and with ML-DSA,

23
00:01:27,791 --> 00:01:32,406
a NIST post-quantum signature, because
these records must stay valid for years.

24
00:01:32,406 --> 00:01:35,098
The contract then mints
10,000 verified tonnes.

25
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:43,126
The mill ships 6,000 tonnes
to an importer in Hamburg,

26
00:01:43,126 --> 00:01:45,434
and 4,000 to one in Rotterdam.

27
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:01,951
Now watch: if it tries to sell
2,000 more 'verified' tonnes,

28
00:02:01,951 --> 00:02:03,874
the smart contract rejects it.

29
00:02:03,874 --> 00:02:05,797
Double counting isn't discouraged here.

30
00:02:05,797 --> 00:02:06,566
It's impossible.

31
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:13,775
When the Hamburg importer files its CBAM
declaration, it surrenders those tonnes.

32
00:02:13,775 --> 00:02:16,852
They're burned, so they
can never be reused.

33
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:25,920
Anyone can open the passport from the
QR code on the shipping documents.

34
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:28,228
It shows who verified the batch,

35
00:02:28,228 --> 00:02:31,689
and checks the post-quantum
signature using chain data alone.

36
00:02:32,039 --> 00:02:35,116
Against the EU default for
Turkish hot-rolled steel,

37
00:02:35,116 --> 00:02:38,578
this one month's output saves
46,000 euros this year,

38
00:02:38,578 --> 00:02:42,424
rising to 2.2 million when
CBAM is fully phased in.

39
00:02:42,774 --> 00:02:45,466
Here's the full trail, block by block.

40
00:02:45,466 --> 00:02:47,005
The original invoice matches.

41
00:02:47,005 --> 00:02:50,851
Change a single figure in
it, and the check fails.

42
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:56,329
Why blockchain?

43
00:02:56,329 --> 00:02:59,406
Producer, verifier and importer
are in different countries,

44
00:02:59,406 --> 00:03:02,098
and the core risk is a double-spend.

45
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:08,905
Under the hood: AI with a human in the
loop, a post-quantum verifier signature,

46
00:03:08,905 --> 00:03:11,597
and a contract that conserves every tonne.

47
00:03:11,947 --> 00:03:15,024
Next: pilots with exporters
in Türkiye and India,

48
00:03:15,024 --> 00:03:18,870
the same data feeding Türkiye's
ETS and India's carbon market,

49
00:03:18,870 --> 00:03:20,408
then cement and fertiliser.

50
00:03:20,408 --> 00:03:23,870
CarbonPass, for the Carbon Markets
and Emissions Transparency track.
